# The Uncanny Valley of Prime-Generating Polynomials

Some formulas look like magic. Euler's polynomial $f(n) = n^2 + n + 41$ produces a
prime for every input from $n = 0$ all the way to $n = 39$ -- forty primes in a
row. It sits at the very edge of plausibility: almost a formula for the primes,
yet not quite. That "almost" is the mathematical uncanny valley.

## Play with the illusion

Set your own coefficients below and watch where the prime run breaks. No matter
how you tune a nonconstant polynomial, a composite value always appears.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Why every such formula must fail

The whole story turns on one identity, the **divisibility engine**:
$$f(a) \mid f\bigl(a + k\,f(a)\bigr).$$
If $f(a) = p$ is prime, then $p$ divides $f(a + k p)$ for every $k$. Each of those
values is supposedly prime and divisible by $p$, so it must equal $\pm p$ -- and a
nonconstant polynomial cannot hit two values infinitely often. Verify the engine
yourself:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

## The valley has infinite width

Failure is not a single pothole. The density of prime outputs drifts toward zero:
composite values eventually dominate. Watch the meter retreat as you widen the
window.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

## The reveal, up close

Euler's run ends exactly at $n = 40$, where $40^2 + 40 + 41 = 1681 = 41^2$.

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Under the hood

The divisibility engine can be turned into a constructive certificate of failure,
and into an estimator for the prime density.

